Likutay Halachos &ndash; Orach Chaim

Hilchos Techumin V&rsquo;Eruvay Techumin &ndash; Halacha 6

The tchum Shabbos is two thousand amos

Oas 1

For the essential holiness of Yisrael is the holiness of Shabbos Kodesh, which is the totality of the entire Torah, as our Sages of blessed memory said. For all the sanctities that Yisrael draw upon themselves through Torah and mitzvos are an aspect of the holiness of Shabbos, as is brought in the holy books. For the holiness of Shabbos one must draw upon oneself throughout all six weekdays, and this is the aspect of &ldquo;Remember the Shabbos day to sanctify it&rdquo; (Shemos 20:8) &mdash; &ldquo;remember it from the first day of the week.&rdquo; That is, through all the Torah and mitzvos that one does throughout the weekdays, through this one draws upon oneself the holiness of Shabbos, which is the totality of the holiness of Yisrael. Therefore one must be very careful with the honor of Shabbos Kodesh, and the main thing is to receive the Shabbos with simchah and with a glad heart, and to distance all types of sadness on Shabbos. Even sadness and worry over one&rsquo;s many sins and transgressions one must push away on Shabbos with all one&rsquo;s strength. For even during the weekdays one must greatly distance sadness, especially at the time when one is engaged in some holiness, in Torah or in a mitzvah &mdash; then one must exert oneself greatly to be in great simchah and not take to heart even worries over sins, but rather to gladden oneself with the little bit of good that one still has, as is explained at length among us regarding this. How much more so on Shabbos Kodesh, when it is forbidden to have any sadness or melancholy whatsoever, for this is the essential holiness of Shabbos &mdash; to rejoice in it with all one&rsquo;s might, for it is a day of simchah and joy in all the worlds, as is written in the holy Zohar, &ldquo;Then joy and delight are heard,&rdquo; etc. For all the holy paths and counsels through which one can come to simchah and joy &mdash; they all awaken and shine on Shabbos with a great light. Only, even so, a person still needs to draw them upon himself, and then on Shabbos he can easily draw upon himself the paths of simchah. And through this he will merit to extend the simchah of Shabbos also into the weekdays, and this is the essential holiness that one extends from Shabbos onto the six weekdays &mdash; namely, the joy of the mitzvah, which is the main thing. For the essential completeness of the mitzvah and its ascent is through simchah, and the essential simchah is drawn from Shabbos onto the weekdays.

Oas 2

For it is found in the words of our master, our teacher and our rebbe, the holy and awesome one, of blessed memory, many counsels and paths to strengthen oneself to gladden one&rsquo;s soul always, through which one will remain standing and never fall, chas v&rsquo;shalom. For this is the essence of everything, as is known. But the greatest source of chizuk is according to what is explained in two holy Toros, which are &ldquo;Azamra l&rsquo;Elokai b&rsquo;odi&rdquo; (Likutay Aleph, siman 282) and what he wrote on the pasuk &ldquo;V&rsquo;ayeh haseh l&rsquo;olah&rdquo; (Likutay Tinyana, siman 12) &mdash; see there well. For in the Torah &ldquo;Azamra l&rsquo;Elokai b&rsquo;odi,&rdquo; etc., it is explained that when a person sees that he is very, very far from Hashem Yisbarach and his deeds are very ugly, nevertheless he should look to search and seek and find within himself some good point, for how is it possible that he never did some mitzvah in all his days? And even though even the mitzvah has much p&rsquo;soles (waste/impurity) in it, nevertheless, at least certainly there is in it some good point. And so he should seek and find in himself yet more good points, etc. And through this he will be able to pray, etc., and this is &ldquo;Azamra,&rdquo; etc. &mdash; see there. And in siman 12 in Likutay Tinyana on the pasuk &ldquo;V&rsquo;ayeh haseh l&rsquo;olah,&rdquo; wondrous things are explained regarding the concept that a descent is ultimately for the purpose of an ascent &mdash; see there very well. And the principle is that the places that are very far from Hashem Yisbarach, which are an aspect of filthy places &mdash; their essential existence is sustained specifically from the aspect of &ldquo;Ayeh m&rsquo;kom k&rsquo;vodo&rdquo; (&ldquo;Where is the place of His glory?&rdquo;), which is the aspect of B&rsquo;raishis, the hidden utterance, from which all the Ten Utterances receive, for it encompasses them all. And from there specifically the very distant places mentioned above receive their vitality, for they receive from the greatness of the concealment and hiddenness of this utterance. And therefore when a person falls there, chas v&rsquo;shalom, and falls into doubts and troubling thoughts and great confusions, and sees the immensity of his distance from Hashem Yisbarach &mdash; he should not fall on account of this, chas v&rsquo;shalom. For on the contrary, through this very thing &mdash; that he sees the greatness of his distance, that he is very far from His glory Yisbarach, and he asks and seeks &ldquo;Ayeh m&rsquo;kom k&rsquo;vodo?&rdquo; etc. &mdash; through this very thing is his essential tikkun and ascent, in the aspect of &ldquo;the descent is ultimately for the ascent.&rdquo; For through the seeking and searching &ldquo;Ayeh?&rdquo; etc., through this he ascends to the aspect of Ayeh in holiness, which is a very exalted level &mdash; the aspect of B&rsquo;raishis, etc. &mdash; from which is the essential existence and vitality of all the Ten Utterances, etc. See all of this there very, very well.

Oas 3

And all these holy paths of simchah and chizuk one must walk in very much, for upon this depends one&rsquo;s essential Yiddishkeit, as is known and understood by anyone who wants to be a true Yisrael forever. However, a person does not merit to walk in them except through the power of the holiness of Shabbos Kodesh, which is the aspect of the power of the true Tzaddik, who is an aspect of Shabbos (as is found in the Zohar, &ldquo;You are the Shabbos of all the days,&rdquo; and as is brought in his words, of blessed memory). For the holy path of &ldquo;Azamra l&rsquo;Elokai b&rsquo;odi&rdquo; mentioned above &mdash; its essential illumination is on Shabbos. For during the weekdays, when the Sitra Achra and the klipos have a hold, as is known, therefore even the good and the mitzvos that one does during the weekdays &mdash; the Sitra Achra has a hold in them, which is the hold of sadness, as is brought in his words, of blessed memory, on the pasuk &ldquo;Tzedek l&rsquo;fanav y&rsquo;halaich,&rdquo; etc. (in siman 139 and in siman 277) &mdash; that the mitzvos done during the weekdays, the Sitra Achra has a hold on the aspect of the &ldquo;feet&rdquo; of the mitzvah, in the aspect of &ldquo;Her feet go down to death,&rdquo; etc. (Mishlei 5:5). And through this the mitzvah cannot walk and glorify itself before Hashem Yisbarach. But when Shabbos arrives, then the &ldquo;feet&rdquo; ascend from the klipah and the Sitra Achra, for this is the essential holiness of Shabbos, in the aspect of &ldquo;If you restrain your feet on Shabbos&rdquo; (Yeshayahu 58:13), and then the mitzvah begins to walk before Hashem Yisbarach, and Hashem Yisbarach delights in it and glorifies Himself with it greatly, even though it was done in great smallness. Nevertheless, from His great love for Yisrael, His close nation, He delights in it greatly and makes from it a paved path, etc. &mdash; see there.

And the matter is explained according to the above, for during the weekdays it is the aspect of a servant, where sadness has a hold (and as is explained in Likutay Tinyana, siman 2, in the Torah &ldquo;The days of Chanukah are days of thanksgiving&rdquo;). Therefore even the mitzvos done then have the hold of sadness in them, and this itself is the aspect of the hold of the Sitra Achra mentioned above that grips the feet of the mitzvah in the aspect of &ldquo;Her feet go down to death.&rdquo; For the essential hold of the Sitra Achra is through sadness, which is the side of death, and this is the essential aspect of the exile of the Shechinah that intensifies during the weekdays (as is explained in the Torah &ldquo;The midpoint of the world,&rdquo; Likutay Aleph, siman 24). And then, from the great intensification of the darkness of sadness, one needs very great and mighty effort to clarify the good in the mitzvos &mdash; that is, to remind oneself of the good points within oneself, to gladden one&rsquo;s soul, to remind oneself: &ldquo;After all, even so, I am doing a mitzvah, and even if it is as it is, at least there are good points in it, for a single point of even a minor mitzvah of the most minor person &mdash; the entire world is not worthy of it,&rdquo; etc. But from the great darkness of the weekdays, which is the intensification of sadness, which is anpin chashochin (dark countenance) &mdash; through this it is very hard to remind oneself of all this and to revive oneself and to gladden oneself. And because of this, sometimes they want to make him fall in his mind completely, reminding him of his many sins and transgressions, and how he is full of blemishes and corruptions at every moment, etc., until it seems to him that hope is lost, chas v&rsquo;shalom. And all this is the work of the Evil One, for this is not the way of humility at all &mdash; rather, the Evil One schemes against him to make him fall completely, chas v&rsquo;shalom. And from the great darkness, it is very hard for him to take to heart and to remind himself of the good points within him &mdash; were it not for Hashem Who helps us every day and at every moment, Who orchestrates through His mighty wonders that we are able to remind ourselves to walk with the holy path of &ldquo;Azamra l&rsquo;Elokai b&rsquo;odi,&rdquo; praise be to G-d &mdash; see there very, very well and you will be able to revive your soul always, all the days of your life.

But all of this holy path is not drawn upon a person except through the power of the holiness of Shabbos. For one must extend the holiness and the simchah of Shabbos upon all the weekdays, and through this one draws upon oneself this holy path in order to revive and gladden one&rsquo;s soul, so that one will remain standing and not leave the boundary and border of holiness entirely, chas v&rsquo;shalom. For the essential illumination of this holy path of &ldquo;Azamra,&rdquo; etc. mentioned above shines in completeness on Shabbos Kodesh. For then the dominion of the Sitra Achra and the chitzonim is nullified, and all the sadness and melancholy are nullified &mdash; they being the aspect of the severity of judgment, in the aspect of &ldquo;And all the masters of wrath and the lords of judgment &mdash; they all flee and are removed from it,&rdquo; etc., &ldquo;and all freedom is found.&rdquo; For then, on Shabbos, great chasadim and boundless compassion are awakened, and then the ways of Hashem and His true kindnesses are revealed and shine in all the worlds &mdash; for His way, Yisbarach, is not to look at the evil of a person but to gaze and look at the good within him, as is written, &ldquo;If I had seen iniquity in my heart, Hashem would not have listened &mdash; but indeed G-d has heard,&rdquo; etc. (Tehillim 66:18-19). And as is written, &ldquo;He did not gaze at iniquity in Yaakov and did not see,&rdquo; etc. (Bamidbar 23:21). And as is explained in siman 17 in Likutay Tinyana, &ldquo;The way of Hashem Yisbarach,&rdquo; etc., which speaks there of the holiness of Shabbos &mdash; see there.

And therefore, then on Shabbos, all the mitzvos of Yisrael begin to walk and glorify themselves before Hashem Yisbarach, for the hold of the klipos &mdash; which is the sadness that grips the feet of the mitzvah &mdash; is nullified from them. And then the preciousness and holiness of the good points of the mitzvos is revealed, until they walk and glorify themselves before Hashem Yisbarach and make a paved path for all, so that every person can draw close to Hashem Yisbarach. And therefore, then on Shabbos the aforementioned path of &ldquo;Azamra l&rsquo;Elokai b&rsquo;odi&rdquo; shines in completeness, because then the Sitra Achra and the klipos cannot cover and conceal the good through the abundance of evil, and cannot cast sadness, chas v&rsquo;shalom. For then only the good shines &mdash; the good points of the mitzvos &mdash; and through this the evil and the sadness fall completely, and then songs, hymns and praises to Hashem Yisbarach are aroused, in the aspect of &ldquo;A psalm, a song for the Shabbos day&rdquo; (Tehillim 92:1), in the aspect of &ldquo;And the seventh day praises and says,&rdquo; etc. For then wondrous songs and hymns are aroused, made and composed from the good points of the mitzvos of all Yisrael &mdash; even of the sinners of Yisrael &mdash; which all ascend then and glorify themselves before Hashem Yisbarach, and from them are made songs and hymns and wondrous melodies. For all of this is the aspect of &ldquo;Azamra l&rsquo;Elokai b&rsquo;odi&rdquo; mentioned above, as is explained there in its place, that wondrous melodies and songs are made from this. But the main thing is accomplished on Shabbos Kodesh, for then is the essential, complete revelation of all the good points of all the mitzvos of all Yisrael, as above. And from Shabbos one must extend the holiness and the simchah upon all the weekdays &mdash; that is, through the power of the holiness of Shabbos, we merit even throughout all the weekdays to walk in this holy path, to drive away sadness and to revive our souls always through the good points that are found in us, which is the path of &ldquo;Azamra l&rsquo;Elokai b&rsquo;odi,&rdquo; as above.

Oas 4

And this is the aspect of what is found in the Midrash: &ldquo;I am black but beautiful&rdquo; (Shir HaShirim 1:5) &mdash; &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; during the weekdays, &ldquo;but beautiful&rdquo; on Shabbos. For this verse &ldquo;I am black but beautiful&rdquo; alludes to the holy path of &ldquo;Azamra&rdquo; mentioned above, and as is explained among us in Hilchos Hashkamas HaBoker, Halacha 1 (see there) &mdash; that the souls of Yisrael say to the nations and the Sitra Achra who want to make them fall, chas v&rsquo;shalom, by showing them their ugly deeds. And then Yisrael respond to them: &ldquo;I am black but beautiful.&rdquo; It is true that I am black with my bad deeds, but &ldquo;beautiful am I&rdquo; with the good points of the mitzvos and good deeds that are still found in me &mdash; as Rashi explains there: &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; with my deeds, &ldquo;but beautiful&rdquo; with the deeds of my forefathers. And even among my own deeds there are some that are beautiful &mdash; if I have the sin of the Golden Calf, I have against it the merit of receiving the Torah. This is as above, for this is the holy path of &ldquo;Azamra l&rsquo;Elokai b&rsquo;odi&rdquo; that our master, our teacher and our rebbe, of blessed memory, revealed.

But this holy path &mdash; its essential illumination is drawn through the holiness of Shabbos, which is the aspect of the holiness of the true Tzaddik, as above. And this is what is found in the Midrash on this verse: &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; with my deeds, &ldquo;but beautiful&rdquo; with the deeds of my forefathers; &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; all the days of the week, &ldquo;but beautiful&rdquo; on Shabbos. And there it further enumerates many more: &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; in Mitzrayim, &ldquo;but beautiful&rdquo; in Mitzrayim; &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; in Mitzrayim &mdash; &ldquo;they rebelled against Me,&rdquo; etc.; &ldquo;but beautiful&rdquo; in Mitzrayim &mdash; with the blood of the Pesach offering and the blood of circumcision, as is written, &ldquo;And I passed over you and I saw you wallowing in your bloods, and I said to you: &lsquo;In your blood, live!&rsquo;&rdquo; (Yechezkel 16:6) &mdash; this is the blood of the Pesach, &ldquo;and I said to you,&rdquo; etc. &mdash; this is the blood of the circumcision. &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; at the Sea, as is said, &ldquo;They rebelled at the Sea,&rdquo; etc. (Tehillim 106:7). &ldquo;But beautiful&rdquo; at the Sea, as is said, &ldquo;This is my G-d and I will beautify Him,&rdquo; etc. (Shemos 15:2). And so it enumerates there many more instances where they sinned greatly, yet in that very place they also merited much good. And all of this is the aspect of the aforementioned holy path of &ldquo;Azamra l&rsquo;Elokai b&rsquo;odi,&rdquo; as above.

But all of this cannot be revealed except through the power of the true Tzaddikim, which is the aspect of the power of the holiness of Shabbos, as above. And this is the aspect of &ldquo;I am black with my deeds, but beautiful with the deeds of the fathers&rdquo; &mdash; who are the aspect of the power of the Tzaddikim, who are the fathers of the world, as is written, &ldquo;My father, my father, the chariot of Yisrael and its riders&rdquo; (Melachim II 2:12). And so it is stated explicitly there in the Midrash: &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; with their deeds that are not good, &ldquo;but beautiful&rdquo; with the deeds of the Tzaddikim &mdash; as it says there: &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; with the spies, &ldquo;but beautiful&rdquo; with Yehoshua and Kalaiv, etc.; &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; at Shittim, etc., &ldquo;but beautiful,&rdquo; as is said, &ldquo;And Pinchas stood and prayed&rdquo; (Tehillim 106:30); &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; with Achan, &ldquo;but beautiful&rdquo; with Yehoshua, etc. And this itself is the aspect of &ldquo;I am black during the weekdays, but beautiful on Shabbos,&rdquo; for the power of Shabbos and all the Tzaddikim are one aspect, as above. For through this specifically one can reveal the good points that exist even in the most lowly of the lowly, to revive his soul so that he remains standing &mdash; and this is the aspect of the holy path of &ldquo;Azamra&rdquo; mentioned above, which is the aspect of &ldquo;I am black but beautiful,&rdquo; as the Midrash goes on to explain all the good that is found in them in the depths of their lowliness and blackness, as above. For it is impossible to reveal all this good from within the depth of the darkness of the abundance of evil that covers it, except through the power of the true Tzaddikim and through the power of Shabbos Kodesh, as above. And now all the words of the aforementioned Midrash are beautifully connected and joined together &mdash; see there well, and you will now see and understand that it all speaks of the matter of the aforementioned holy path of &ldquo;Azamra l&rsquo;Elokai b&rsquo;odi,&rdquo; which is the aspect of &ldquo;I am black but beautiful,&rdquo; as above.

And this is the aspect of what is written there: &ldquo;I am black&rdquo; in the wilderness, etc.; &ldquo;but beautiful&rdquo; in the wilderness, with the erection of the Mishkan. For the Mishkan was built from all the good points found in each and every member of Yisrael. And see all of this in our words in Hilchos Hashkamas HaBoker, Halacha 1 (see there).

Oas 5

And likewise the holy path that he revealed on the pasuk &ldquo;V&rsquo;ayeh haseh l&rsquo;olah&rdquo; (&ldquo;And where is the lamb for the offering?&rdquo;) (B&rsquo;raishis 22:7) &mdash; see there well and set your heart well to understand the words, especially to understand them practically, to internalize the subtle, deep, true counsels, to fulfill them with t&rsquo;mimus and with simplicity. And this path, too, is drawn essentially only through the holiness of Shabbos Kodesh and the holiness of the true Tzaddik, which are one aspect, as above. For the essential matter explained there is that even when a person falls, chas v&rsquo;shalom, to places that are very, very far from Hashem Yisbarach, which are the aspect of filthy places, Rachmana litzlan, nevertheless there is no despair in the world at all. For even in these places, even though they are very far from His glory, Yisbarach, in the aspect of &ldquo;And My glory I will not give to another&rdquo; (Yeshayahu 42:8), as is explained there &mdash; nevertheless, if he will search and seek His glory, because he sees the greatness of his distance from His glory, Yisbarach, and he will be pained and will yearn and will search and seek: &ldquo;Ayeh m&rsquo;kom k&rsquo;vodo?&rdquo; &mdash; through this itself the descent will be transformed into a great ascent, for he will ascend to the aspect of Ayeh in holiness, which is a very, very exalted level. For Ayeh is the hidden utterance, which is above everything, and all the Utterances, which are the aspect of the revelation of His glory, Yisbarach &mdash; they all receive from there. And from it specifically the distant places receive their vitality &mdash; they being the aspect of filthy places &mdash; for from the aspect of the revealed Utterances where His glory, Yisbarach, is revealed, the filthy places cannot receive any vitality. For there is a boundary to every glory that is clothed in each and every one of the Ten Utterances, that it should not spread and go out to these places, chas v&rsquo;shalom, in the aspect of &ldquo;And My glory I will not give to another.&rdquo; But the aspect of Ayeh, the hidden utterance, which is a very exalted and concealed level &mdash; from the greatness of its concealment and hiddenness, from there specifically the distant places mentioned above receive. And therefore when a person falls there, chas v&rsquo;shalom, when he searches and seeks &ldquo;Ayeh m&rsquo;kom k&rsquo;vodo?&rdquo; because he is far from His glory, as above &mdash; through this specifically he ascends to the ultimate ascent, for he ascends to the aspect of Ayeh in holiness, which is a very exalted level, which is the root of everything, from which all the Utterances receive &mdash; they being the revelation of His glory through which the entire world was created. See all of this there well.
